          "derivation": "Pass-through node: the Greek box is printed empty in the book (only the header 'Greek  SKT:87'), so the extraction is faithful — no word was lost. The word Nourai means is Byzantine (medieval) Greek τζυκάνιον tzykánion 'the game of polo, mall', with the verb τζυκανίζειν 'to play at mall' and τζυκανιστήριον, the polo ground built beside the Great Palace in Constantinople. It was taken over from Middle Persian čawgān/čōkān when the Sasanian court game reached Byzantium; the Iranian affricate č- is rendered by the Greek cluster τζ-, the regular treatment of č in Greek loans from Iranian, and the ending was Hellenised with the neuter diminutive -ιον. Skeat s.v. chicanery states the same: 'chicane is from the medieval Gk. …, a word of Byzantine origin …, from Pers. chaugān, a club, bat', and Lokotsch p.35 no.434: 'Nach Byzanz gelangt, hieß das Spiel mgr. τζυκάνιον, Vb. τζυκανίζειν, \"Spielplatz dafür\" τζυκανιστήριον'.",
